Data lanes are the pipeline itself, left to right, in order.
Invoke connections are capabilities you hand to a node. An agent does not pass data "into" a model in sequence, it calls the model whenever it needs to think, as many times as it likes. Same with a tool: the agent calls it when it decides it needs a web page.
That is why they hang underneath rather than sitting in the flow.
A lane is a pipe. An invoke connection is a phone number.

4. The .pipe file format
JSON. The key you always need is components, the array of nodes. Each has an id, a provider (the node type, like webhook or llm_openai), a config, and optionally input (data connections in) and control (invoke connections in).
This is exactly the canvas, written down.input is the solid lines, control is the dashed ones.
A webhook straight to a response, the smallest thing that runs:

{
 "components": [
 { "id": "in", "provider": "webhook", "config": {} },
 {
 "id": "out",
 "provider": "response",
 "config": { "laneName": "text" },
 "input": [{ "lane": "text", "from": "in" }]
 }
 ]
}

5. Local or Cloud
The same .pipe file runs in both places, unchanged. You are changing one dropdown.
In VS Code, open the RocketRide extension settings, Development tab, Connection mode.
Option 1: Local
The extension downloads and manages the engine for you. Leave Server Version on <Latest>. Turn on Full debug output when stuck, then read Output → RocketRide: Console.
Connecting locally also writes ROCKETRIDE_URI and ROCKETRIDE_APIKEY into your .env, which you need in section 6. So connect through the extension once before writing any code.

Option 2: RocketRide Cloud
Pick RocketRide Cloud in the same dropdown, then:
Sign in. A green tick and your name confirm it
Choose your Team, which decides which team's engine you connect to
Save All Settings, top right. It does not apply until you do
That is it. The identical pipeline now runs on Cloud instead of your laptop.

Build on Local, switch to Cloud for the demo. A judge opening a live link beats you screen sharing localhost.
If you type a Cloud address by hand it must be https:// or wss://. Write http:// or ws:// and the connection silently downgrades, with no error. This applies to Cloud only. ws://localhost:5565 for a local engine is correct.

6. Running from your own code
The moment your project has a UI or a script, you need the SDK.

pip install rocketride python-dotenv

import asyncio, os
from dotenv import load_dotenv
from rocketride import RocketRideClient

load_dotenv()

async def main():
 async with RocketRideClient(
 uri=os.environ['ROCKETRIDE_URI'],
 auth=os.environ['ROCKETRIDE_APIKEY'],
 ) as client:
 result = await client.use(filepath='hello.pipe')
 token = result['token']
 try:
 out = await client.send(token, 'Hello, pipeline!',
 objinfo={'name': 'input.txt'},
 mimetype='text/plain')
 print(out)
 finally:
 await client.terminate(token)

asyncio.run(main())
Four calls: connect, use() to start and get a token, send() to push a payload, terminate() to stop. To run against Cloud, change nothing in the code, point .env at Cloud instead. Use client.pipe() for streaming large data.
terminate is not optional. The async with block calls disconnect(), but that only drops the WebSocket, the pipeline keeps running on the engine. Leave it out and every run leaves a live pipeline behind. We hit this internally and ended up with four orphans nobody could account for.
TypeScript SDK has the same shape.

