Part 3 · Choose your Million dollar App Idea to Build from or come up with something
Every one is a full agentic system: it ingests continuously, keeps memory that compounds, takes real action with a human gate where the stakes demand it, and has a buyer with an existing budget line.
Judging bar: load-bearing use. A one-line SDK import that is never called again does not count.


2. Bid Factory 
For B2B companies that respond to RFPs and tenders with dedicated teams. Monitors procurement portals and incoming RFPs, and keeps a growing answer library - every past Q&A, win/loss result, pricing call, and compliance document - all searchable. Reads each new RFP, matches requirements to existing answers, writes what's missing, builds the full response with a compliance checklist, and schedules reviews - with multiple tools working together and humans approving each section and the final submission. Revenue: per-seat subscription (high contract value); each RFP is a large, billable workload.

3. Churn Rescue Desk 
For SaaS companies trying to keep their revenue from shrinking. Constantly reads product usage, billing activity, support tone, and when key contacts change jobs; tracks what healthy vs. churned accounts looked like at each stage and which actions saved which type of customer. Scores churn risk continuously, picks the right response playbook, and runs it - writes the outreach email, creates a task for the customer success manager, applies pre-approved discounts within set limits - humans approve anything above a certain threshold, and every result improves the playbook. Revenue: % of saved recurring revenue or a monthly per-account fee; always-on scoring means constant compute usage.

4. AP Payment Fraud Sentinel 
For mid-size finance teams paying hundreds of vendors, blocking email fraud and fake bank-detail changes. Checks every invoice, vendor detail update, and approval against a database of vendors, bank accounts, approvers, and past payment behavior - the patterns fraud hides inside. Automatically holds anything suspicious and verifies it out-of-band, including calling the vendor on their known number rather than any number in a suspicious email; a human must manually release every held payment. Revenue: fee per invoice screened plus % of losses prevented; one blocked six-figure fraud pays for years of the product.

5. Compliance Change Radar 
For compliance teams at banks, fintechs, and insurers working across multiple countries. Watches regulator announcements and maintains the client's compliance register as a connected map - each regulation linked to the internal policies, controls, and owners that implement it. Every new regulatory update is compared against that map to find exactly which policies are affected; the system drafts the required change, creates a ticket for the responsible owner, and keeps the audit trail regulators will want - compliance officer approves every change. Revenue: per-country subscription against one of B2B's most stable budget lines; runs on a regular schedule.

6. Field Claims Copilot 
For insurance companies and claims processors handling property and car damage. Takes in claim submissions, field photos and videos, and weather/disaster data; maintains a record linking policy details to claim history, known fraud patterns, and local repair cost benchmarks. Assesses damage severity from all the evidence, generates a repair estimate, books inspections via calendar and automated phone calls, flags potential fraud, and prepares the settlement recommendation - a licensed human adjuster approves every settlement. Revenue: priced per claim; photos, voice, and documents together make this a heavy, well-monetized workload.

7. Warranty & Returns Arbiter 
For electronics and appliance brands overwhelmed by return and warranty requests. Takes in return requests, device diagnostic data, customer photos/videos of faults, and retailer data; builds a product-wide fault map showing which parts fail, in which production batches, under which conditions, alongside each customer's purchase and claim history. Decides repair/replace/refund/deny with supporting evidence, issues return labels and authorizations, sends all denials to human review, and spots patterns early ("this batch's hinge failure rate just tripled") before they turn into recalls. Revenue: fee per return processed plus an upsell for fault intelligence; constant device data ingestion is steady volume.
1. Chargeback Defender 
For online stores and SaaS businesses losing money to payment disputes. Tracks dispute alerts, orders, user sessions, and delivery data; builds a per-customer record linking all of these plus past dispute results. When a new dispute comes in, it gathers evidence from all systems, packages it in the format the payment processor needs, and submits it before the deadline - a human checks before every submission - then records the result so future wins and losses improve the process. Revenue: % of money recovered or a fee per dispute; every dispute is a paid job.

8. Media Rights Sentinel 
For content owners - studios, labels, course creators, sports bodies - losing licensing money to unauthorized use. Scans new uploads, marketplace listings, and broadcast feeds; keeps a content fingerprint database linked to license agreements, regions, and enforcement history, so a legitimate licensee is never confused with a pirate. Matches what it finds against the license database, estimates the value of each violation, and takes action - takedown, licensing offer, or legal escalation pack - with a human approving every outbound claim. Revenue: % of licensing revenue recovered; fingerprint matching at scale shows off the system's parallel processing power.

9. Global Workforce Compliance Guard 
For companies hiring contractors and remote workers across many countries. Reads timesheets, invoices, contracts, and employment law updates per country; maintains a connected record of each worker, their contract, their country, and their classification, with full history. Continuously re-checks misclassification risk as laws and work patterns shift, drafts contract updates, keeps the filing calendar per country, and builds the audit-defense file - country-specific agents coordinating under a human legal gate on every change. Revenue: per-worker monthly fee against a serious risk (misclassification fines); scheduled re-checks are recurring compute.

10. Ad Spend Autopilot 
For performance marketing teams managing significant monthly budgets across channels. Tracks live campaign results, pacing, and how each creative is performing; keeps a record of which message, format, and audience combination worked - so that knowledge survives team changes and platform switches. Shifts budget within set limits, pauses underperforming ads, writes the next creative brief based on proven results, and sends the weekly attribution report - with analysts, a budget tool, and a brand-safety reviewer involved, plus human approval above spend-change limits. Revenue: % of managed spend or tiered budget fees; always-on optimization means constant usage.


11. Denial Recovery Desk 
For hospital and clinic billing teams losing 3-5% of revenue to insurance denials. Reads payment files, clinical notes, prior authorization records, and insurance policy updates; keeps a record of which argument, with which clinical evidence, actually overturned which denial code at which insurer. For each denial it builds the appeal with exactly the right supporting documents, files before the insurer's deadline, and escalates to a doctor-to-doctor review when the data shows written appeals won't win - a coder or clinician approves every clinical claim. Revenue: % of recovered revenue, the standard model in this space; every denial is a billable job and denial volume never drops.

12. Deduction Defender 
For consumer brand finance teams losing 1-2% of revenue to retailer chargebacks and unauthorized deductions. Reads payment remittances, promotion agreements, shipment data, and retailer compliance manuals; builds a record that learns which retailer takes which invalid deduction and which dispute package gets money returned. Checks every deduction against the agreement that supposedly authorized it, auto-disputes invalid ones with proof attached, and writes off valid ones without wasting human time - deductions above a set amount need analyst approval. Revenue: % of recovered deductions plus a per-retailer subscription; every remittance file is heavy recurring volume.

13. Change Order Claims Desk 
For contractors and subcontractors who lose margin because delay and change claims are filed late or without solid evidence. Reads daily site reports, RFIs, submittals, schedule updates, weather data, and site photos; builds a connected record linking every disruption to the contract clause that allows recovery and the evidence that proves it. When a delay happens it drafts the notice within the contractual deadline - where most claims die - builds the entitlement case with dated evidence, and prices the impact - the project manager approves every submission. Revenue: % of recovered claim value or a per-project fee; constant site document ingestion across a portfolio is sustained compute.

14. Lease Audit Engine 
For retail, restaurant, and enterprise tenants overpaying landlords on operating expense bills that nobody has time to review. Reads lease documents, annual expense reconciliation statements, landlord invoices, and property tax records; keeps a per-site record linking every clause, obligation, and billed amount - so an exclusion agreed in a 2019 amendment still gets enforced in the 2026 bill. Recalculates what should have been charged, flags overcharges with the exact clause cited, drafts the dispute letter, and tracks recovery to close - a real estate lead approves every letter to the landlord. Revenue: % of recovered overcharges, the standard model for lease audit firms; every reconciliation cycle across a portfolio is a large batch job.

15. Revenue Leakage Guard 
For B2B SaaS finance and RevOps teams quietly under-billing because contracts and billing setup drift apart over time. Reads signed contracts, billing system config, entitlement records, and product usage data; maintains a record tracking what each customer agreed to, what they were given access to, what they actually used, and what they were charged. Continuously compares all four, raises catch-up billing proposals where usage exceeded entitlements, flags things provisioned but never billed, and catches expired discounts still being applied - finance approves every customer-facing adjustment. Revenue: % of recovered leakage or a subscription tiered by annual revenue; continuous reconciliation means always-on usage.

16. Grant Compliance Manager 
For universities, hospitals, and nonprofits managing restricted funding where one disallowed expense becomes a repayment demand. Reads grant agreements, expense and payroll data, effort certifications, and funder policy updates; keeps a record of every grant, its restrictions, every expense charged to it, and whether it was allowed - with full history so the same cost type is never judged differently across grants. Checks every transaction against the specific grant's rules, blocks or flags disallowed spend before it posts, builds financial and progress reports by funder deadlines, and prepares the audit-defense file - the grants officer approves every determination. Revenue: per-grant monthly subscription against a serious audit risk; constant transaction screening plus scheduled reporting.

17. Utility Spend Auditor 
For businesses running many sites - retail chains, manufacturers, logistics networks - quietly paying the wrong electricity or gas rate across dozens of meters. Reads utility bills across suppliers and formats, meter interval data, published rate schedules, and contract terms; keeps a per-site record of every meter, its correct rate, its usage, and every past claim outcome. Recalculates each bill against the rate the site should actually be on, finds billing errors, overcharges, demand-charge savings, and unbilled or double-billed meters - then files refund claims with the utility and tracks them to credit, with facilities approving anything that changes a contract. Revenue: % of savings and recovered credits; monthly bill processing across hundreds of meters is steady batch volume.

18. Franchise Standards Auditor 
For franchise brands whose reputation depends on hundreds of independently run locations they can only physically visit twice a year. Reads location-submitted photos and videos, inspection reports, review and complaint feeds, and point-of-sale signals; keeps a record per location of every standard, every violation, and every fix - showing which issues keep coming back, which get fixed fast, and which signal a franchisee heading toward termination. Scores each location continuously, schedules re-inspections based on risk rather than a fixed calendar, sends cure notices with evidence attached, and escalates repeat violations into a formal default package - a franchise manager approves every notice. Revenue: per-location monthly subscription; constant photo and video processing across a network is exactly the heavy workload this system monetizes best.

19. IP Docket Guard 
For in-house IP lawyers and patent firms where one missed deadline permanently kills an asset worth more than the firm. Reads patent office updates across countries, docketing records, renewal fee schedules, and internal product portfolio data; maintains a connected record of every patent family, country, deadline, and business value - so renewal decisions are made based on what the asset is actually worth, not just by default. Tracks every legal deadline, drafts office action responses using the patent's own filing history, and escalates renewal decisions with a business case attached rather than silently paying or letting patents lapse - attorney sign-off required on every filing. Revenue: per-asset annual subscription against catastrophic risk; portfolio-wide monitoring runs continuously.

20. Safety Reporting Command 
For EHS managers at industrial and construction sites where filing a regulatory report late is itself a violation. Reads incident and near-miss reports including field voice notes, inspection findings, equipment sensor data, and corrective action records; keeps a connected record of every hazard, control, incident, and fix across all sites - so a near-miss pattern at one plant automatically flags the same unguarded hazard at eleven others. Classifies each incident against reporting thresholds, files the regulatory report within the legal window, assigns and follows up on corrective actions until closed, and surfaces which sites are trending toward a serious event - the EHS lead approves every regulatory filing. Revenue: per-site monthly subscription; constant incident data ingestion plus scheduled reporting.

